in the global race for coronavirus  vaccines  biontech pfizer was first out of the  gate  a proud achievement for biontech the  german company that went from startup to  pharma superstar  now in the debate over waving patents on  covet 19 vaccines  the german government is siding with  drug makers saying  the protection of intellectual property  is a source of innovation  and must remain so in the future the  limiting factor in the production of  vaccines are production capacities and  the high quality standards  not the patents germany says  industry and government should work on  transferring technology to new  manufacturers  if they want to scale up vaccine  production quickly  and in a jab at the biden administration  german health minister yen span had this  to say  if the real problem isn't patents but  production capacity and availability  then i'd be delighted if the united  states is now willing to allow the  export of doses manufactured there  just like the european union  the us is just one of the wealthy  nations accused of vaccine hoarding  extending their immunization programs  far beyond their vulnerable populations  while poorer nations go without the  pharma industry says  that problem should be fixed first  but it's pictures like these from crisis  hit india  which have brought the problem of  vaccine shortfalls into sharp focus  prompting more than a hundred countries  including now the u.s  to support patent waivers as a way to  boost global supply  for many global health advocates it's  the right way forward  companies have earned a tremendous  amount in this this year alone on  coveted vaccines 7 billion us dollars  per pfizer  that seems like more than a sufficient  amount to be investing in innovation in  the future  moreover patent protection didn't  deliver the vaccines that we have now  significant government intervention and  government investment  from across the world has done that for  us  in india meanwhile the pace of  vaccinations has fallen  sharply for these people help from the  global community can't come soon enough  joining me now is stefan lieber he's a  member of parliament for the opposition  socialist left party  uh mr libby what do you think about the  german government's move to refuse  a patent waiver  i think this is a very very bad decision  if we look at our neighbors it's a  polish government or as a french  government  or at the secretary generals of the  world health organization  or of the un general secretary antonio  guterres  everyone is supporting this waiver only  the german government  is on the side of the pharmaceutical  industry  i don't think that this is a very wise  decision well critics say a waiver  would have very little immediate impact  because of bottlenecks in manufacturing  and and delivery of vaccines  what impact do you think a waiver would  actually have  we have seen this this report from india  a few minutes ago and the indian  government  was asking begging on the side of south  africa  for such a waiver they are not all  stupid people there and over a hundred  governments are asking for this waiver i  don't think  that the german government is much wider  if it is only bringing  a little bit to help this covet crisis  we should do it  as a trade representative of the u.s  government catherine  taylor has said we are in  extraordinary circumstances and this  needs extraordinary measures  we are supporting the us government but  our government  doesn't the german government said  intellectual property is a source of  innovation  pharma companies have invested billions  into developing these  do they have a point  yes but the german public the german  taxpayers  have invested a lot in those companies  so we have helped them to invent all  these very very important vaccines  and we don't want to cancel intellectual  property rights at all  it is just about the covit 19 vaccines  and i think this would be a very  a good and very important step well  pfizer is already  of selling their vaccine cheaper to low  income countries on a non-profit basis  don't you think that's enough  i'm on the side of india of south africa  of over 100 countries of important  organizations like  doctors without borders and so on i  think  you could back and wait to  do some gifts from companies but you can  make decisions too and this is what  states are about and this is what the  world trade organization is about  and i i really cannot imagine that the  german government would block  such uh such an action if the u.s  government is supporting it  stephen a member of the member of  parliament  for the opposition socialist left party  thank you very much  thank you and joining me me now for more  on this is thomas queenie he's the  director general of the  ifpma that's the global association of  pharmaceutical research  companies are you opposing this patent  waiver  and if so why  the patent waiver wouldn't really  address the problem because when you  look at the images speed from  india and nepal which we have just seen  we need immediate action and immediate  action the best we can do right now is  we need more solidarity from the rich  countries with the poor countries  because of the indian emergency  situation  supplies from indian manufacturers to  kovacs the global solidarity initiative  have trickled and we need to act fast  what we see right now is you have the  u.s and some other rich countries now  vaccinating healthy young  adults and in africa we have almost  nothing  therefore we need those shares from  which countries europe started  de vevo wouldn't give you a single  edition dose right now  because the bottlenecks are different  and we are deeply conscious of  doing everything we can leaving no stone  unturned to expand capacity  and by the way to work with the  developing country  vaccine manufacturers we have 275  partnership deals we have more than 200  technology transfer i've  just 10 days ago held a media conference  with the leaders of the  indian vaccine manufacturers and they  said the same as we did  the focus on patents is basically  ill-conceived  because it wouldn't address the need for  know-how transfer for  ensuring that those manufacturing paid  the vaccines  do have the skill sets  pfizer has already sold 3.5 billion  dollars  worth of this vaccine and critics are  saying  um pfizer and other drug makers have  made enough  uh alrea enough sales already through  this  pandemic wouldn't you agree  this is not about you know making money  when i look at we have had  several of the largest vaccine  manufacturers like msd  like sanofi like gsk they don't have a  vaccine  yet which means you had companies like  fisa bioentec  succeeding johnson and johnson as far  zenica and what they are doing is acting  socially responsible they do sell  these vaccines at not for profit or at  forecast or even below cost  to developing countries that's what we  need to do they do  team up with partners in developing  countries  we do have new partnership for example  with the company like bayer or novartis  they were not even in the vaccine  business they have now come  to help out and that's what's happening  we have  convened co-convened a global vaccine  and supply chain manufacturing summit  where we looked at what are the problems  the problems are the trade barriers  starting by the way with the united  states with the defense production act  which hinders exports of ingredients to  india  to problems of scarcity of raw materials  and it's not  quite true that you know the private  sectors just benefited  from public money when you look at  bioentec or modern or the two with  messenger r  day technology this goes back 30 years  and they got a lot of confidence  thomas queenie there the director  general of the global association  of pharmaceutical research companies  many thanks  thank you

